  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Access control",
      "items": [
        "user_groups is a pure join table: its primary key is (user_id, group_id), so a membership cannot be duplicated.",
        "permissions grants one capability on one project to either a user or a group; both columns are nullable and exactly one is filled.",
        "groups.name is unique because SSO reconciles IdP groups by name, not by id.",
        "users carries a partial unique index on (oidc_issuer, oidc_subject): the SSO identity anchor, null for local accounts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Project catalog",
      "items": [
        "projects nests through parent_id, a self-reference not drawn as an arrow; RBAC is checked against the project.",
        "flow_versions keeps immutable definition history, unique on (flow_id, version).",
        "connections and datasources are each unique on (project_id, name), so names only have to be unique inside a project.",
        "A datasource can come from a flow, from a database connection, or from neither: all three columns are nullable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Execution",
      "items": [
        "runs.parent_run_id is a self-reference, not drawn: a parent run fans out into child runs.",
        "runs is also the orchestration queue: a PENDING row is claimed by one orchestrator (claimed_by) with a conditional update and kept alive by heartbeat_at.",
        "runs.datasource_id and datasources.snapshot_run_id point at each other, so snapshot_run_id is a plain integer with no foreign key to avoid a circular constraint.",
        "started_at includes queue time; engine_started_at records when the engine actually began, and drives the staleness clock.",
        "pending_blob_deletions deliberately has no foreign key: it must survive the rows whose objects it deletes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Foreign keys listed, not drawn",
      "items": [
        "Ownership and actor columns all point at users.id: projects.owner_id, flows.owner_id, connections.owner_id, datasources.owner_id, saved_views.owner_id, runs.launched_by, flow_versions.created_by, ai_models.updated_by, privacy_notice.updated_by, disabled_engines.disabled_by.",
        "Scheduling actors: flows.run_scheduled_by, datasources.refresh_scheduled_by. Failure notices: flows.notify_connection_id points at connections.id.",
        "Publish target: runs.publish_project_id points at projects.id. saved_views.project_id points at projects.id like every catalog object.",
        "Self-references: projects.parent_id and runs.parent_run_id.",
        "All 35 foreign keys are nullable except the project_id columns, so deleting a user never cascades away history."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Replicas and contracts",
      "items": [
        "login_attempts, flow_presence and schema_state moved out of process memory so the gateway can run in several copies; the scheduler sweeps the expired rows.",
        "schema_state holds a fingerprint of the applied schema: a start-up applies DDL only when it differs, behind an advisory lock.",
        "data_contracts, data_contract_versions and data_contract_results carry no foreign key on purpose: SQLite does not enforce them in tests, and the rows are removed explicitly when the datasource goes.",
        "data_contract_results.notify is the outbox of the alerts: pending rows are sent by the scheduler, claimed one at a time."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
